<h1>Conveyor Suppliers</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Sourcing conveyor equipment involves navigating a supply chain that includes OEM manufacturers, system integrators, dealers, distributors, and aftermarket parts suppliers. Each channel serves a different purpose: manufacturers design and build equipment, integrators deliver turnkey systems, distributors stock components for quick delivery, and parts suppliers keep existing systems running. This guide explains who supplies what, when to use each type of supplier, and how to get quotes that can be compared. For specific company listings, see our <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">manufacturer directory</a>. For finding suppliers in your region, see <a href="/resources/conveyor-companies/">conveyor companies near you</a>.</p>
<h2 id="chain-heading">Types of Conveyor Suppliers</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Supplier Type</th>
<th>What They Supply</th>
<th>Best For</th>
<th>Typical Lead Time</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>OEM Manufacturer</td>
<td>Design and build conveyor equipment in their own factory</td>
<td>Complete conveyors, custom engineering, direct factory support</td>
<td>2-16 weeks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>System Integrator</td>
<td>Turnkey conveyor systems with design, equipment, controls, and installation</td>
<td>Multi-conveyor systems, PLC integration, <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automation</a></td>
<td>12-24 weeks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Dealer / Rep</td>
<td>Specific manufacturer product lines with local sales support</td>
<td>Purchasing a known brand, warranty coordination, regional service</td>
<td>2-12 weeks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Industrial Distributor</td>
<td>Components from multiple manufacturers, local inventory</td>
<td><a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">Parts</a>, motors, drives, bearings, quick delivery</td>
<td>Same-day to 2 weeks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Belting Supplier</td>
<td>Conveyor belting in standard and custom sizes</td>
<td><a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">Belt replacement</a>, fabricated belt lengths, custom materials</td>
<td>Same-day to 2 weeks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Aftermarket Parts</td>
<td>Replacement rollers, bearings, guides, and wear components</td>
<td><a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">Maintenance</a> supplies, cross-referenced parts, MRO inventory</td>
<td>Same-day to 1 week</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 id="when-heading">When to Use Each Supplier Type</h2>
<h3>Buy Direct from an OEM Manufacturer When...</h3>
<ul>
<li>•You need a single conveyor with custom specifications (non-standard length, width, or features)</li>
<li>•You have in-house engineering to handle controls integration</li>
<li>•You want direct access to the manufacturer's application engineers</li>
<li>•You are standardizing on a single conveyor brand across multiple facilities</li>
<li>•The manufacturer has a quick-ship program for standard products</li>
</ul>
<h3>Use a System Integrator When...</h3>
<ul>
<li>•The project includes multiple conveyor types or zones that must work together</li>
<li>•PLC controls, sensors, and <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automation</a> are required</li>
<li>•You need installation, commissioning, and startup support</li>
<li>•You lack internal resources for project management and coordination</li>
<li>•The conveyor system must integrate with robots, packaging machines, or warehouse management systems</li>
</ul>
<h3>Use a Distributor When...</h3>
<ul>
<li>•You need components quickly and cannot wait for manufacturer lead times</li>
<li>•You are sourcing standard parts (motors, VFDs, bearings, rollers) from multiple brands</li>
<li>•Your maintenance team builds MRO inventory for in-house repairs</li>
<li>•You want consolidated billing and a single purchasing relationship for multiple products</li>
<li>•Local inventory and emergency delivery capability matter</li>
</ul>
<h3>Use a Belting Supplier When...</h3>
<ul>
<li>•You need <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">replacement belts</a> fabricated to specific lengths</li>
<li>•You want to evaluate alternative belt materials (upgrade from PVC to polyurethane, for example)</li>
<li>•Your existing conveyor manufacturer no longer supports the equipment or has long lead times</li>
<li>•You need on-site belt splicing or installation services</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="new-heading">Sourcing New Conveyor Systems</h2>
<h3>1. Define Requirements Before Contacting Suppliers</h3>
<p>Suppliers cannot provide accurate quotes without clear specifications. At minimum, define: product dimensions and weight range, required throughput (units per minute or feet per minute), environment conditions (temperature, humidity, washdown, cleanroom), integration points with upstream and downstream equipment, controls scope (standalone, integrate with existing PLC, or new controls package), and installation scope (equipment only, rigging, full installation). See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for factors that affect pricing.</p>
<h3>2. Request Quotes from Multiple Suppliers</h3>
<p>Get quotes from 2-4 suppliers to understand the market. Send the same specification document to each supplier to ensure comparable quotes. Include different supplier types if appropriate: one OEM manufacturer, one integrator, and one regional dealer can show how different business models price the same scope. Allow 1-2 weeks for suppliers to respond with detailed quotes on custom systems; simple catalog products may quote within days.</p>
<h3>3. Compare Scope, Not Just Price</h3>
<p>Quote comparison requires checking what is included: Is the price for equipment FOB factory, or delivered to site? Is installation included, and what does installation mean (mechanical only, or electrical and controls)? What is the warranty duration and coverage? Are startup, commissioning, and operator training included? Are spare parts or maintenance contracts quoted? A lower-priced quote that excludes installation, startup, and warranty may cost more in total than a higher-priced turnkey quote.</p>
<h3>4. Evaluate the Supplier, Not Just the Equipment</h3>
<p>Check supplier capabilities beyond the quoted equipment: Do they have experience in your industry? Can they provide references from similar projects? What is their service territory and response time for emergency calls? Do they stock spare parts? What are their payment terms? How do they handle change orders during the project? A conveyor is a multi-year asset, and the supplier relationship extends well beyond the initial purchase. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-companies/">company evaluation guide</a> for detailed criteria.</p>
<h2 id="parts-heading">Sourcing Parts &amp; Components</h2>
<h3>OEM Parts vs. Aftermarket Alternatives</h3>
<p>For critical components that affect conveyor performance or safety, OEM parts ensure compatibility and maintain warranty coverage. However, many conveyor components are standard industrial items available from multiple sources. Rollers, bearings, motors, VFDs, and belting are often interchangeable across conveyor brands. Ask your conveyor manufacturer what percentage of parts are proprietary versus standard catalog items. Proprietary parts must come from the OEM; standard parts can be sourced competitively from distributors.</p>
<h3>Major Industrial Distributors</h3>
<p>Large industrial distributors stock conveyor components from multiple manufacturers and offer technical support, same-day shipping on stocked items, and consolidated purchasing. Motion Industries, Applied Industrial Technologies, Grainger, and McMaster-Carr carry rollers, bearings, motors, drives, belting, and conveyor accessories. Regional distributors often provide faster local delivery and may specialize in specific industries or product categories. Set up accounts with one or two distributors so <a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">maintenance</a> purchasing isn't a fresh negotiation every time.</p>
<h3>Specialty Parts Suppliers</h3>
<p>Some components require specialty suppliers. Belting suppliers fabricate custom belt lengths and can source specialty materials (high-temperature, food-grade, antistatic). Roller manufacturers build replacement rollers to match existing conveyors. Sprocket and chain suppliers provide precision-matched components for chain-driven conveyors. Guide rail and side frame suppliers stock profiles compatible with major conveyor brands. For obsolete or discontinued conveyors, specialty suppliers may reverse-engineer parts that the original manufacturer no longer supports.</p>
<h3>Building MRO Inventory</h3>
<p>For production-critical conveyors, stock essential spare parts to minimize downtime. Typical MRO (maintenance, repair, operations) inventory includes: drive belts, replacement conveyor belts, rollers (sample of each size used), bearings, motors (or motor rebuild kits), VFDs, sensors and photoeyes, chain links and master links, guide rails, and emergency stop switches. Work with your conveyor manufacturer to identify recommended spare parts and their reorder points. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">parts guide</a> for component details.</p>
