<p>Lead times fluctuate with manufacturer capacity, supply chain conditions, and order complexity. The ranges above represent typical conditions; confirm current lead times when requesting quotes. For production-critical projects, consider quick-ship programs from manufacturers like Dorner, mk North America, and Hytrol that maintain inventory of standard products.</p>
<h2 id="rfq-heading">RFQ Best Practices</h2>
<h3>What to Include in Your RFQ</h3>
<ul>
<li>•<strong>Product specifications:</strong> Dimensions (L x W x H), weight range, material, surface finish, bottom surface</li>
<li>•<strong>Throughput requirements:</strong> Units per minute, feet per minute, surge capacity</li>
<li>•<strong>Layout:</strong> Conveyor length, width, height, curves, inclines, floor plan or CAD if available</li>
<li>•<strong>Environment:</strong> Temperature range, humidity, washdown requirements, cleanroom class, outdoor exposure</li>
<li>•<strong>Controls:</strong> Standalone, integrate with existing PLC (specify make/model), new controls package</li>
<li>•<strong>Power:</strong> Available voltage, phase, amperage at installation location</li>
<li>•<strong>Scope:</strong> Equipment only, equipment + installation, turnkey including commissioning</li>
<li>•<strong>Timeline:</strong> Required delivery date, installation window, production start date</li>
<li>•<strong>Documentation:</strong> Required deliverables (drawings, manuals, spare parts lists, training)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Common RFQ Mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>•<strong>Vague specifications:</strong> "We need a conveyor for boxes" forces suppliers to guess and results in non-comparable quotes</li>
<li>•<strong>Unclear scope:</strong> Not specifying whether installation, controls, or commissioning are included</li>
<li>•<strong>Unrealistic timeline:</strong> Expecting custom equipment in 2 weeks, then being surprised by the actual lead time</li>
<li>•<strong>Price-only evaluation:</strong> Choosing the lowest price without comparing scope, warranty, and supplier capability</li>
<li>•<strong>Too many suppliers:</strong> Requesting quotes from 10+ suppliers creates evaluation fatigue and supplier disengagement</li>
</ul>
<h3>Negotiation Points Beyond Price</h3>
<ul>
<li>•<strong>Extended warranty:</strong> Negotiate from standard 1-year to 2-year coverage, or include wear parts</li>
<li>•<strong>Spare parts package:</strong> Include recommended spares in the initial order at a discount</li>
<li>•<strong>Maintenance contract:</strong> Lock in preventive maintenance pricing for 2-3 years</li>
<li>•<strong>Payment terms:</strong> Milestone-based payments rather than 100% upfront</li>
<li>•<strong>Performance guarantee:</strong> Throughput or uptime guarantees with remedies for non-performance</li>
<li>•<strong>Future expansion pricing:</strong> Locked pricing for additional equipment ordered within 12-24 months</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What types of conveyor suppliers are there?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Conveyor suppliers fall into several categories: OEM manufacturers who design and build equipment, distributors who stock components from multiple brands, dealers/reps who sell specific manufacturer product lines, system integrators who design and install complete conveyor systems, and aftermarket parts suppliers who provide replacement components. Each plays a different role in the supply chain, and the right choice depends on whether you need a complete system, a single conveyor, or replacement parts.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Should I buy conveyors from a manufacturer or distributor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>For complete conveyor systems requiring custom engineering, buying direct from a manufacturer or through a system integrator typically provides better results. Manufacturers offer deeper application engineering support and can customize designs for your specific requirements. For standard components, replacement parts, and commodity items like belting or rollers, distributors often provide faster delivery from local stock and competitive pricing. Many buyers use both: manufacturers for new conveyor systems and distributors for ongoing parts and consumables.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do I get quotes from conveyor suppliers?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Prepare a specification document that includes product dimensions and weight, required throughput, environment conditions (temperature, washdown, cleanroom), layout constraints, and controls requirements. Send the same specification to 2-4 suppliers to get comparable quotes. Include timeline requirements if urgent. Be specific about what you need quoted: equipment only, equipment plus installation, or a complete turnkey system. Vague requests result in quotes that cannot be compared.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What is the typical lead time for conveyor equipment?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Lead times vary widely by conveyor type, customization level, and supplier capacity. Standard catalog conveyors from manufacturers with quick-ship programs can ship in 1-3 weeks. Custom-engineered conveyors typically require 8-16 weeks from order to shipment. Complete conveyor systems with controls integration may require 12-24 weeks including design, fabrication, and FAT (factory acceptance testing). Component suppliers and distributors often stock common parts for same-day or next-day shipping.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">How do I find reliable conveyor parts suppliers?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Start with the OEM manufacturer of your existing conveyor for critical components and proprietary parts. For standard components (rollers, bearings, motors, VFDs, belting), industrial distributors like Motion Industries, Applied Industrial Technologies, and Grainger stock products from multiple manufacturers. Specialty suppliers focus on specific components: belting suppliers stock multiple belt types and can fabricate custom lengths, while roller manufacturers supply replacement rollers in standard and custom sizes. Ask your conveyor manufacturer what percentage of parts are standard catalog items versus proprietary.</p>

</div>
</div>
